<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A dusty smell the first time you turn on the heat each fall? Totally normal in Texas. A sharp electrical or melting-plastic smell is not. Shut it off at the service switch and call for help.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Repairs That Should Be Done by a Professional</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Some furnace repairs look easy on YouTube. In real life, they need tools, training and a license most homeowners don’t have.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Leave these to a tech:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Gas valve replacement or adjustment.</strong> The wrong gas pressure can cause overheating, soot or carbon monoxide.</li>



<li><strong>Ignitor and control board replacement.</strong> Parts have to match exactly, and it’s easy to misdiagnose.</li>



<li><strong>Inducer motor and pressure switch repairs.</strong> These control how combustion gases get vented out of your house.</li>



<li><strong>Blower motor replacement.</strong> It involves capacitors that can hold a dangerous charge even with the power off.</li>



<li><strong>Heat exchanger inspection.</strong> Cracks are often invisible without special tools.</li>



<li><strong>Vent and flue repairs.</strong> Bad venting is a leading cause of CO problems.</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">There’s a legal side too. In Texas, heating and air conditioning work is licensed through the Texas Department of Licensing and Regulation (TDLR). Hiring a licensed contractor protects you. And DIY or unlicensed repairs on internal parts can void your manufacturer’s warranty.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">The Heat Exchanger and Carbon Monoxide</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The heat exchanger is what keeps combustion gases away from the air you breathe. Over the years, all that heating and cooling can crack it, and a cracked heat exchanger can leak carbon monoxide into your ductwork.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You can’t see or smell CO. Exposure can cause headaches, dizziness, nausea and fatigue. A big red flag is several people in the house feeling sick at the same time, then feeling better once they leave.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Here’s how to protect your household:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>Put CO detectors on every floor and near the bedrooms.</li>



<li>Test them every month, and swap the batteries when you change the clocks.</li>



<li>Replace them by the date printed on the back.</li>



<li>Get a yearly furnace inspection that includes a heat exchanger check.</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Older furnaces, and ones that have overheated again and again from poor airflow, are the most at risk. Yet another reason those filter changes matter.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Winter Prep: Get Ready Before the First Cold Front</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The best furnace repair is the one you never need. A few weeks before it gets cold (usually October in North Texas), run through this list:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>Change the air filter.</li>



<li>Move storage boxes away from the furnace, especially in the attic.</li>



<li>Make sure furniture or rugs aren’t blocking vents and return grilles.</li>



<li>Test your CO and smoke detectors.</li>



<li>Turn the heat on for a test run before you actually need it.</li>



<li>Book a professional tune-up.</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A tune-up usually covers cleaning the burners and flame sensor, testing the safety controls, checking gas pressure, inspecting the heat exchanger and making sure everything’s venting right.</p>



